1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,265
You light the
barbecue.

2
00:00:01,265 --> 00:00:02,405
Summer's long
evenings

3
00:00:02,405 --> 00:00:03,495
have vanished.

4
00:00:03,900 --> 00:00:05,240
Earth keeps
spinning,

5
00:00:05,240 --> 00:00:07,300
but its axis
has no tilt.

6
00:00:07,300 --> 00:00:09,750
No hemisphere leans
toward the sun.

7
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:10,880
June.

8
00:00:10,880 --> 00:00:11,740
December.

9
00:00:11,740 --> 00:00:12,580
You still
get roughly

10
00:00:12,580 --> 00:00:14,240
twelve daylight
hours.

11
00:00:14,240 --> 00:00:15,790
Your calendar
can't help.

12
00:00:16,900 --> 00:00:18,740
So you search for
summer south.

13
00:00:18,740 --> 00:00:19,900
Same problem.

14
00:00:19,900 --> 00:00:20,980
Neither
hemisphere gets

15
00:00:20,980 --> 00:00:22,430
those long
evenings.

16
00:00:22,700 --> 00:00:24,520
Same hours
of sun.

17
00:00:24,520 --> 00:00:25,740
But low
rays spread

18
00:00:25,740 --> 00:00:27,430
light over
more ground.

19
00:00:28,200 --> 00:00:29,340
Try the equator.

20
00:00:29,340 --> 00:00:30,620
Noon sun
overhead;

21
00:00:30,620 --> 00:00:32,120
still no
late sunset.

22
00:00:32,120 --> 00:00:34,500
Polar sunlight
skims the horizon.

23
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:35,640
January brings
slightly

24
00:00:35,640 --> 00:00:37,640
stronger sunlight
worldwide,

25
00:00:37,640 --> 00:00:39,480
thanks to our
oval orbit.

26
00:00:39,480 --> 00:00:40,240
It can't restore

27
00:00:40,240 --> 00:00:41,900
long summer
evenings.

28
00:00:41,900 --> 00:00:42,760
Winter's shortest

29
00:00:42,760 --> 00:00:44,060
afternoons
are gone,

30
00:00:44,060 --> 00:00:44,980
too.

31
00:00:44,980 --> 00:00:46,450
Your barbecue
survives.

32
00:00:46,780 --> 00:00:48,180
Move dinner
earlier;

33
00:00:48,180 --> 00:00:49,990
the calendar
can't save it.
